// WHERE A SHOP TAKES PAYMENT FROM (12 October), the four questions shop.rs2 used to answer with
// inv_total/inv_del/oc_name against %shop_currency directly.
//
// Every shop in the build before this one was paid out of the player's inventory - coins for 38 of
// them, Tokkul for the three TzHaar traders, Marks of Grace for Grace - so "the currency" and
// "where the currency is" were the same question and %shop_currency answered both. The vote shop is
// the first where they come apart: a vote point is a varp, not an obj, because a vote TICKET is
// tradeable and the entire reason to redeem one is that what you get back cannot be traded, dropped
// or lost. See vote/scripts/vote_ticket.rs2 for the model.
//
// WHY THIS COUPLES THE SHOP SYSTEM TO ONE FEATURE. There is no way in this dialect to hold a varp in
// a variable and read it back, so a generic "the balance lives in varp N" is not expressible: the
// branch has to name %vote_points. One `if` in four procs is the whole cost, and it is visible here
// rather than smeared through shop.rs2.
//
// A POINTS SHOP NEVER PAYS OUT. ~can_sell_obj refuses everything at one, so there is no
// ~shop_wallet_gain and no path that can add points - a shop that could buy items for vote points
// would be a point faucet. Selling is the inventory wallet's business only.

// What to call it in a message. "gp" is not usable here: the inventory wallet says "coins",
// "tokkul" or "marks of grace" depending on the shop, and the points wallet has no obj to name.
[proc,shop_wallet_name]()(string)
if (%shop_wallet = ^shop_wallet_vote_points) {
    return("vote points");
}
return(lowercase(oc_name(%shop_currency)));

[proc,shop_wallet_total]()(int)
if (%shop_wallet = ^shop_wallet_vote_points) {
    return(%vote_points);
}
return(inv_total(inv, %shop_currency));

[proc,shop_wallet_spend](int $amount)
if (%shop_wallet = ^shop_wallet_vote_points) {
    %vote_points = calc(%vote_points - $amount);
    return;
}
inv_del(inv, %shop_currency, $amount);

// THE EXACT-CHANGE EXEMPTION, and the reason it is a proc rather than a condition written inline.
// ~buy_item lets a purchase through with no free inventory slot when the cost comes to exactly what
// you are holding, because the coin stack is about to empty and free the slot it was in. Nothing
// empties when the wallet is a varp, so that exemption has to be false there - and a points balance
// that happens to equal the price would otherwise trip it and buy an item with nowhere to put it.
[proc,shop_wallet_exact_change](int $cost, int $held)(boolean)
if (%shop_wallet = ^shop_wallet_vote_points) {
    return(false);
}
if ($cost = $held) {
    return(true);
}
return(false);
